        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The principal objective of this research proposal is to understand how mechanical loads imposed by gravity are translated into developmental programs that maintain structural integrity of the plant body.
<P>
APPROACH:<br>
Specifically, we will investigate the role of the plasma membrane receptor-like kinase FERONIA in this process, as we have recently discovered that FERONIA is crucial to normal mechanical signaling and mechanical development of the model plant Arabidopsis thaliana (Mouse Ear Cress). We will employ fluorescence microscopy, growth kinematics, molecular biology, and genomic approaches to investigate the impact of variable gravity conditions on plant architecture development and to define how FERONIA interacts with mechanical signal transduction pathways.
<P>
RESULTS:<br>
Cellular perception of mechanical stress is of fundamental importance to living systems. In plants, environmental mechanical stresses shape plant architecture from the cellular level to the level of organ formation. We have previously identified a receptor-like kinase, FERONIA, as a key regulator of mechanical Ca2+ signaling and control element of root growth responses to environmental mechanical stresses and turgor-driven cellular expansion. We have now further found that FER is also an important regulator of seedling shoot responses to mechanical perturbation. Whether FER functions as a true mechanosensor remains to be established, but current evidence suggests that FER is directly involved in the early phases of mechanical signal transduction.

We have developed computer-vision-assisted assays to monitor how mechanical signaling affects the mechanical properties of cell walls in living, intact organs at subcellular spatial and second temporal resolution.
        </div>

